{
  "schema": "ld-s10y-image/figure-spec@2",
  "id": "fig-63",
  "mode": "deterministic",
  "description": "Instructional schematic for tracing staged AC electrical-energy transmission and branching distribution, preserving all source transformer stages, paired connections, generator/user labels, and 12 source voltage labels. These source example levels are not universal standards.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "/Users/yong/.codex/worktrees/stemrobin-181-six/lemmadeck/ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/10p/figures/fig-63.png",
      "sha256": "976cb5467dcf363266da274817d6f7918ce21d6f019575ea4dc563b5504010c6"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:zh",
        "text": "电能的用户分布在不同地点，而电站可能建在靠近燃料或水力资源的地方。\n电站与用户之间的距离使远距离输电成为必要，输电并不是因为电能绝对不能储存。\n电流通过有电阻的输电导线时，一部分电能转化为热。\n在导线电阻和电流有效值保持不变的情况下，焦耳—楞次定律给出这部分能量：\n$Q=I^2Rt$。\n这里 Q 是转化为热的电能，I 是电流有效值，t 是通电时间；对恒定直流，I 就是恒定电流的大小。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:zh",
        "text": "其中 $R$ 是输电导线的电阻。\n相同材料和截面积的导线越长，电阻越大；能量损耗的经济影响还取决于所输送的功率等条件。\n增大导线截面积可以减小电阻，但会增加材料用量和成本。\n因此，降低输电电流是减小导线发热损耗的重要办法。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:zh",
        "text": "在交流输电中，当输送的有功功率、功率因数和线路接法保持不变时，提高电压有效值可以降低电流有效值，从而减小导线发热损耗。\n长距离输电常采用高电压，但电压的选择还要考虑绝缘、线路结构和成本。\n比较电压层级时，可以取输电电压为 $500$ 千伏、发电机端电压为 $16$—$20$ 千伏的示例。\n这些数值不是所有系统的固定标准，后一范围也不是交流发电机电压的普遍上限。\n更高电压对绕组和其他部件的绝缘设计提出更高要求。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:zh",
        "text": "电站可以利用升压变压器把发电机端的交流电压提高到适合输电的电压。\n对于不计损耗的理想变压器，在输入与输出的功率因数相同、接法对应的条件下，输出电压比输入电压提高多少倍，输出电流就比输入电流减小多少倍。\n实际变压器存在绕组发热和铁芯损耗等，输出有功功率小于输入有功功率，不能把理想关系当作无条件成立的精确结果。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:zh",
        "text": "车床电动机、照明线路和其他用电设备需要与设备额定值相适应的电压，而不是直接使用远距离输电的高电压。\n在交流供电线路的末端，可以利用降压变压器把电压降低到合适的层级。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:zh",
        "text": "输电电压通常分几个阶段降低，线路也逐步分支，把电能送往分布在各处的用户。\n在所输送的功率和功率因数相同的比较中，降低电压对应着增大电流；各条分支的电流还取决于各自负载，不能认为所有下游导线中的电流都会逐级增大。\n电能输送和分配的示意图见图 $63$。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:zh",
        "text": "图 63"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:zh",
        "text": "电压很高时，导线表面附近的空气可能发生电晕放电，造成额外的能量损耗。\n电晕的出现与导线表面附近的电场有关，导线粗细和布置等都会影响它。\n选择交流线路的电压时，需要考虑电压峰值，并通过适当的导线和绝缘设计控制电晕损耗，而不是假定高压线路没有这种损耗。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:zh",
        "text": "高压直流输电是一种可用于远距离输送电能的方案，但不是在所有条件下都优于交流输电。\n在简化比较中，如果允许的直流工作电压可以取到交流电压的峰值，并且允许的电流和其他限制条件可比，直流线路可能具有较大的输送功率；仅凭使用相同导线不能保证这一结果。\n在稳态直流输电中，没有交流线路的感抗压降和持续的电容充放电电流，这对长距离线路具有意义。\n电感和电容仍然存在，并在电流或电压变化时起作用；直流输电仍有导线、电晕和换流设备等损耗。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:zh",
        "text": "在一种交流电站接入直流输电线路的方案中，先用变压器调整交流电压，再用整流器把交流电转换为直流电。\n在受电端，用逆变器把直流电转换为交流电，并通过变压器调整到所需的交流电压。\n整流器和逆变器都是换流设备；换流需要相应的设备、控制和绝缘设计，也会带来损耗，不能用普通变压器直接改变稳态直流电压。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:zh",
        "text": "用高压输电线连接不同地区的电站和用户，可以形成互联电网，也就是由发电、输电和用电设施共同组成的电力系统。\n互联电网通过协调发电和跨地区输电，可以在条件允许时调配电能，帮助应对早晨、晚上等时段的负荷高峰。\n它不能消除所有负荷高峰，也不能保证电能不受地点和线路状况影响而总能送达；供电仍受线路容量、损耗、设备运行状态和故障等限制。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:en",
        "text": "Electricity users are spread across different locations, while power stations may be built near fuel supplies or water resources.\nThe distance between stations and users creates a need for long-distance transmission; this does not mean that electrical energy can never be stored.\nWhen current flows through a transmission conductor with resistance, some electrical energy is converted to heat.\nFor constant conductor resistance and a constant root-mean-square current, the Joule–Lenz law gives this energy as:\n$Q=I^2Rt$.\nHere Q is the electrical energy converted to heat, I is the root-mean-square current, and t is the elapsed time; for steady direct current, I is simply the constant current."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:en",
        "text": "The quantity $R$ is the resistance of the transmission conductor.\nFor the same material and cross-sectional area, a longer conductor has greater resistance; the economic effect of its losses also depends on conditions such as the transmitted power.\nIncreasing the conductor's cross-sectional area reduces its resistance but requires more material and adds cost.\nReducing the transmission current is therefore an important way to reduce resistive heating losses."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:en",
        "text": "In AC transmission, increasing the root-mean-square voltage reduces the root-mean-square current when the transmitted active power, power factor, and circuit configuration remain unchanged, thereby reducing resistive heating losses.\nHigh voltage is often used for long-distance transmission, but its selection must also account for insulation, line construction, and cost.\nAs an example of voltage levels, consider a transmission voltage of $500$ kilovolts and a generator terminal voltage of $16$–$20$ kilovolts.\nThese values are not fixed standards for every system, and the latter range is not a universal upper limit on AC generator voltage.\nHigher voltages place greater demands on the insulation of windings and other components."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:en",
        "text": "A power station can use a step-up transformer to raise the generator's AC terminal voltage to a suitable transmission voltage.\nFor an ideal transformer with no losses, equal input and output power factors, and corresponding circuit connections, the output current is reduced relative to the input current by the same factor by which the output voltage is increased relative to the input voltage.\nReal transformers have losses, including winding heating and core losses, so their output active power is less than their input active power; the ideal relationship is not an exact result that applies without conditions."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:en",
        "text": "Lathe motors, lighting circuits, and other electrical equipment need voltages suited to their ratings, rather than the high voltage used for long-distance transmission.\nAt the receiving end of an AC supply, step-down transformers can reduce the voltage to an appropriate level."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:en",
        "text": "Transmission voltage is often reduced in several stages, while the network branches to deliver electrical energy to users in different locations.\nFor the same transmitted power and power factor, a lower voltage corresponds to a higher current; each branch current also depends on its own load, so the current does not necessarily increase in every downstream conductor.\nFigure $63$ shows a schematic of electrical energy transmission and distribution."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:en",
        "text": "Figure 63"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:en",
        "text": "At high voltage, corona discharge may occur in the air near a conductor's surface, causing additional energy losses.\nCorona depends on the electric field near the surface and is affected by factors such as conductor size and arrangement.\nSelecting an AC line voltage requires attention to the peak voltage and suitable conductor and insulation design to control corona losses, rather than assuming that a high-voltage line has none."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:en",
        "text": "High-voltage direct-current transmission is an option for transmitting electrical energy over long distances, but it is not superior to AC transmission under every set of conditions.\nIn a simplified comparison, if the permitted DC operating voltage can equal the peak AC voltage and the current limits and other constraints are comparable, a DC line may carry more power; using the same conductors alone does not guarantee this result.\nSteady DC transmission avoids the inductive-reactance voltage drop and continuing capacitive charging current associated with AC lines, which matters for long-distance lines.\nInductance and capacitance still exist and affect changes in current or voltage; DC transmission also retains conductor, corona, and converter losses."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:en",
        "text": "In one arrangement for connecting an AC power station to a DC transmission line, a transformer first adjusts the AC voltage, and a rectifier then converts AC to DC.\nAt the receiving end, an inverter converts DC back to AC, and a transformer adjusts the AC voltage to the required level.\nRectifiers and inverters are converter equipment; conversion requires suitable equipment, control, and insulation design and introduces losses, while an ordinary transformer cannot directly change a steady DC voltage."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c1-s4-n35:en",
        "text": "Connecting power stations and users in different regions with high-voltage transmission lines can form an interconnected grid: a power system comprising generation, transmission, and electricity-use facilities.\nBy coordinating generation and transmission between regions, an interconnected grid can redistribute electrical supply when conditions permit and help manage demand peaks, such as those in the morning and evening.\nIt cannot eliminate every demand peak or guarantee delivery regardless of location and line conditions; supply remains limited by line capacity, losses, equipment operating conditions, and faults."
      }
    ],
